<p><b>Tides</b> are the rising of <a href=3D"/wiki/Earth" title=3D"Earth=
">Earth</a>'s <a href=3D"/wiki/Ocean" title=3D"Ocean">ocean</a> surface =
caused by the <a href=3D"/wiki/Tidal_force" title=3D"Tidal force">tidal =
forces</a> of the <a href=3D"/wiki/Moon" title=3D"Moon">Moon</a> and the=
 <a href=3D"/wiki/Sun" title=3D"Sun">Sun</a> acting on the oceans.<sup i=
d=3D"cite_ref-Reddy_0-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"#cite_note-Reddy=
-0"><span>[</span>1<span>]</span></a></sup><sup id=3D"cite_ref-Jain_1-0"=
 class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"#cite_note-Jain-1"><span>[</span>2<span>=
]</span></a></sup><sup id=3D"cite_ref-Hubbard_2-0" class=3D"reference"><=
a href=3D"#cite_note-Hubbard-2"><span>[</span>3<span>]</span></a></sup> =
Tides cause changes in the depth of the marine and <a href=3D"/wiki/Estu=
ary" title=3D"Estuary">estuarine</a> water bodies and produce oscillatin=
g currents known as tidal streams, making prediction of tides important =
for coastal navigation (see <i><a href=3D"/wiki/Tide#Navigation" title=3D=
"Tide">Navigation</a></i>). The strip of seashore that is submerged at h=
igh tide and exposed at low tide, the <a href=3D"/wiki/Intertidal_zone" =
title=3D"Intertidal zone">intertidal zone</a>, is an important ecologica=
l product of ocean tides (see <i><a href=3D"/wiki/Tide#Intertidal_ecolog=
y" title=3D"Tide">Intertidal ecology</a></i>).</p>
<p>The changing tide produced at a given location is the result of the c=
hanging positions of the Moon and Sun relative to the Earth coupled with=
 the <a href=3D"/wiki/Coriolis_effect" title=3D"Coriolis effect">effects=
 of Earth rotation</a> and the <a href=3D"/wiki/Bathymetry" title=3D"Bat=
hymetry">bathymetry</a> of oceans, seas and estuaries.<sup id=3D"cite_re=
f-3" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"#cite_note-3"><span>[</span>4<span>]=
</span></a></sup> Besides the ocean, tidal <a href=3D"/wiki/Phenomena" t=
itle=3D"Phenomena" class=3D"mw-redirect">phenomena</a> can occur in othe=
r systems whenever a gravitational field that varies in time and space i=
s present (see <i><a href=3D"/wiki/Tide#Other_tides" title=3D"Tide">Othe=
r tides</a></i>).</p>
<p>The daily sea-level fluctuations, as they actually occur =E2=80=94 es=
pecially in shallow seas and near coasts =E2=80=94 are not solely caused=
 by the <a href=3D"/wiki/Astronomy" title=3D"Astronomy">astronomical</a>=
 tidal forcing, but may also be strongly affected by <a href=3D"/wiki/Me=
teorology" title=3D"Meteorology">meteorology</a> (wind and barometric pr=
essure), for instance resulting in <a href=3D"/wiki/Storm_surge" title=3D=
"Storm surge">storm surges</a>.</p>
