<h2><span class=3D"editsection">[<a href=3D"/w/index.php?title=3DTide&am=
p;action=3Dedit&amp;section=3D1" title=3D"Edit section: Characteristics"=
>edit</a>]</span> <span class=3D"mw-headline">Characteristics</span></h2=
>
<div class=3D"thumb tright">
<div class=3D"thumbinner" style=3D"width:302px;"><a href=3D"/wiki/File:T=
ide_type.gif" class=3D"image" title=3D"Fig. 1: Types of tides"><img alt=3D=
"" src=3D"http://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/0/00/Tide_type.gif" w=
idth=3D"300" height=3D"415" class=3D"thumbimage" /></a>
<div class=3D"thumbcaption">
<div class=3D"magnify"><a href=3D"/wiki/File:Tide_type.gif" class=3D"int=
ernal" title=3D"Enlarge"><img src=3D"/skins-1.5/common/images/magnify-cl=
ip.png" width=3D"15" height=3D"11" alt=3D"" /></a></div>
<i>Fig. 1:</i> Types of tides</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>A tide is a repeated cycle of sea level changes in the following stag=
es:</p>
<ul>
<li>Over several hours the water rises or advances up a beach in the flo=
od</li>
<li>The water reaches its highest level, called high water.</li>
<li>The sea level lowers or falls over several hours during the ebb tide=
.</li>
<li>The level stops falling at low water.</li>
</ul>
<p>The moment that the tidal current ceases, is called <a href=3D"/wiki/=
Slack_water" title=3D"Slack water">slack water</a> or slack tide. Then t=
he tide reverses direction and is said to be turning. Slack water usuall=
y occurs near high water and low water. But there are locations where th=
e moments of slack tide differ significantly from those of high and low =
water.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-4" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"#cite_note-4=
"><span>[</span>5<span>]</span></a></sup></p>
<p>Tides may be semidiurnal (two high waters and two low waters each day=
), or diurnal (one tidal cycle per day). In most locations, tides are <i=
>semidiurnal</i>. Because of the <i>diurnal</i> contribution, there is a=
 difference in height (the daily inequality) between the two high waters=
 on a given day; these are differentiated as the <i>higher high water</i=
> and the <i>lower high water</i> in <a href=3D"/wiki/Tide_table" title=3D=
"Tide table">tide tables</a>. Similarly, the two low waters each day are=
 referred to as the <i>higher low water</i> and the <i>lower low water</=
i>. The daily inequality changes with time and is generally small when t=
he Moon is over the <a href=3D"/wiki/Equator" title=3D"Equator">equator<=
/a>.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-5" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"#cite_note-5">=
<span>[</span>6<span>]</span></a></sup></p>
<p>The various frequencies of <a href=3D"/wiki/Orbital_forcing" title=3D=
"Orbital forcing">orbital forcing</a> which contribute to tidal variatio=
ns are called <a href=3D"/wiki/Earth_tide#Tidal_constituents" title=3D"E=
arth tide">tidal constituents</a>. In most locations, the largest is the=
 "principal lunar semidiurnal" constituent, also known as the <i>M2</i> =
(or <i>M</i><sub>2</sub>) tidal constituent. Its period is about 12 hour=
s and 25.2 minutes, exactly half a <i>tidal lunar day</i>, the average t=
ime separating one lunar <a href=3D"/wiki/Zenith" title=3D"Zenith">zenit=
h</a> from the next, and thus the time required for the Earth to rotate =
once relative to the Moon. This is the constituent tracked by simple <a =
href=3D"/wiki/Tide_clock" title=3D"Tide clock">tide clocks</a>.<sup id=3D=
"cite_ref-6" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"#cite_note-6"><span>[</span>=
7<span>]</span></a></sup></p>
<p>Tides vary on timescales ranging from hours to years, so to make accu=
rate records <a href=3D"/wiki/Tide_gauge" title=3D"Tide gauge">tide gaug=
es</a> measure the water level over time at fixed stations which are scr=
eened from variations caused by waves shorter than minutes in period. Th=
ese data are compared to the reference (or datum) level usually called <=
a href=3D"/wiki/Mean_sea_level" title=3D"Mean sea level" class=3D"mw-red=
irect">mean sea level</a>.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-7" class=3D"reference"><a =
href=3D"#cite_note-7"><span>[</span>8<span>]</span></a></sup></p>
<p>Constituents other than <i>M</i><sub>2</sub> arise from factors such =
as the gravitational influence of the Sun, the tilt of the Earth's rotat=
ion axis, the inclination of the lunar orbit and the ellipticity of the =
orbits of the Moon about the Earth and the Earth about the Sun. Variatio=
ns with periods of less than half a day are called harmonic constituents=
. Long period constituents have periods of days, months, or years.</p>
<p><a name=3D"Range_variation:_springs_and_neaps" id=3D"Range_variation:=
_springs_and_neaps"></a></p>
<h3><span class=3D"editsection">[<a href=3D"/w/index.php?title=3DTide&am=
p;action=3Dedit&amp;section=3D2" title=3D"Edit section: Range variation:=
 springs and neaps">edit</a>]</span> <span class=3D"mw-headline">Range v=
ariation: springs and neaps</span></h3>
<table class=3D"wikitable" align=3D"left" width=3D"300">
<tr align=3D"center">
<td><a href=3D"/wiki/File:Spingtide.jpg" class=3D"image" title=3D"Spingt=
ide.jpg"><img alt=3D"" src=3D"http://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/comm=
ons/thumb/0/03/Spingtide.jpg/150px-Spingtide.jpg" width=3D"150" height=3D=
"150" /></a><br />
<i>Fig. 2:</i> Artist's conception of spring tide</td>
<td><a href=3D"/wiki/File:Neaptide.jpg" class=3D"image" title=3D"Neaptid=
e.jpg"><img alt=3D"" src=3D"http://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/common=
s/thumb/c/c2/Neaptide.jpg/150px-Neaptide.jpg" width=3D"150" height=3D"15=
0" /></a><br />
<i>Fig. 3:</i> Artist's conception of neap tide</td>
</tr>
</table>
<p>The semidiurnal tidal range (the difference in height between high an=
d low waters over about a half day) varies in a two-week or fortnightly =
cycle. Around <a href=3D"/wiki/New_moon" title=3D"New moon">new</a> and =
<a href=3D"/wiki/Full_moon" title=3D"Full moon">full moon</a> when the S=
un, Moon and Earth form a line (a condition known as <a href=3D"/wiki/Sy=
zygy" title=3D"Syzygy">syzygy</a>), the <a href=3D"/wiki/Tidal_force" ti=
tle=3D"Tidal force">tidal forces</a> due to the Sun reinforce those of t=
he Moon. The tide's range is then maximum: this is called the <i>spring =
tide</i>, or just <i>springs</i> and is not named after the <a href=3D"/=
wiki/Spring_(season)" title=3D"Spring (season)">season</a> but, like tha=
t word, derives from an earlier meaning of "jump, burst forth, rise" as =
in a natural <a href=3D"/wiki/Spring_(hydrosphere)" title=3D"Spring (hyd=
rosphere)">spring</a>. When the Moon is at first quarter or third quarte=
r, the Sun and Moon are separated by 90=C2=B0 when viewed from the Earth=
, and the forces induced by the Sun partially cancel those of the Moon. =
At these points in the lunar cycle, the tide's range is minimum: this is=
 called the <i>neap tide</i>, or <i>neaps</i> (a word of uncertain origi=
n). Spring tides result in high waters that are higher than average, low=
 waters that are lower than average, <i><a href=3D"/wiki/Slack_water" ti=
tle=3D"Slack water">slack water</a></i> time that is shorter than averag=
e and stronger tidal currents than average. Neaps result in less extreme=
 tidal conditions. There is about a seven day interval between springs a=
nd neaps.</p>
<p>The changing distance of the Moon from the Earth also affects tide he=
ights. When the Moon is at <a href=3D"/wiki/Perigee" title=3D"Perigee" c=
lass=3D"mw-redirect">perigee</a> the range is increased, and when it is =
at <a href=3D"/wiki/Apogee" title=3D"Apogee" class=3D"mw-redirect">apoge=
e</a> the range is reduced. Every 7=C2=BD <a href=3D"/wiki/Lunation" tit=
le=3D"Lunation">lunations</a>, perigee coincides with either a new or fu=
ll moon causing <a href=3D"/wiki/Perigean_spring_tide" title=3D"Perigean=
 spring tide">perigean spring tides</a> with the largest <i>tidal range<=
/i>. If a storm happens to be moving onshore at this time, the consequen=
ces (in the form of property damage, etc.) can be especially severe.</p>=

<p><a name=3D"Semidiurnal_range_differences" id=3D"Semidiurnal_range_dif=
ferences"></a></p>
<h3><span class=3D"editsection">[<a href=3D"/w/index.php?title=3DTide&am=
p;action=3Dedit&amp;section=3D3" title=3D"Edit section: Semidiurnal rang=
e differences">edit</a>]</span> <span class=3D"mw-headline">Semidiurnal =
range differences</span></h3>
<div class=3D"thumb tright">
<div class=3D"thumbinner" style=3D"width:602px;"><a href=3D"/wiki/File:T=
ide_effects.svg" class=3D"image" title=3D"Dual range variation"><img alt=
=3D"" src=3D"http://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/f/f9/Tide_ef=
fects.svg/600px-Tide_effects.svg.png" width=3D"600" height=3D"421" class=
=3D"thumbimage" /></a>
<div class=3D"thumbcaption">
<div class=3D"magnify"><a href=3D"/wiki/File:Tide_effects.svg" class=3D"=
internal" title=3D"Enlarge"><img src=3D"/skins-1.5/common/images/magnify=
-clip.png" width=3D"15" height=3D"11" alt=3D"" /></a></div>
Dual range variation</div>
</div>
</div>
<p><sup class=3D"noprint Template-Fact" title=3D"This claim needs refere=
nces to reliable sources from July 2009" style=3D"white-space:nowrap;">[=
<i><a href=3D"/wiki/Wikipedia:Citation_needed" title=3D"Wikipedia:Citati=
on needed">citation needed</a></i>]</sup></p>
<p>Gravitational forces on Earth tides of the Earth:Moon:Sun system are =
in the ratio of 893:11:5 taking account of distances to their respective=
 centres of gravity. Considering the Earth-Moon system as a binary plane=
t, its centre of gravity is 4634&#160;km from the centre of the Earth in=
 the direction of the Moon. This direction varies continuously during th=
e day and during the synodic month following the <a href=3D"/wiki/Orbit_=
of_the_Moon" title=3D"Orbit of the Moon">Orbit of the Moon</a>. In place=
s experiencing semidiurnal tides, near the solstices this gives rise to =
two significantly different sequential high tide levels that vary indepe=
ndently. The reason for this can be followed in the accompanying diagram=
. Water at point A is attracted by gravity towards the centre of the Ear=
th and towards the Sun in one direction and towards the Moon in the othe=
r. Twelve hours later it is located at A' where it is attracted towards =
the sun in one direction and towards the centre of gravity of the binary=
 Earth-Moon system in the other. The distance to the binary CoG is about=
 11,000&#160;km. instead of 6380&#160;km to the centre of the Earth. As =
gravity is acting from a greater distance on A' its force is reduced by =
the ratio of the reciprocal squares of distance. This gravity force dimi=
nution causes high tides to develop on opposite sides of the Earth simul=
taneously. It is this combination that gives rise to different high tide=
 levels at twelve hourly intervals.<sup class=3D"noprint Template-Fact" =
title=3D"This claim needs references to reliable sources from July 2009"=
 style=3D"white-space:nowrap;">[<i><a href=3D"/wiki/Wikipedia:Citation_n=
eeded" title=3D"Wikipedia:Citation needed">citation needed</a></i>]</sup=
></p>
<p><a name=3D"Phase_and_amplitude" id=3D"Phase_and_amplitude"></a></p>
<h3><span class=3D"editsection">[<a href=3D"/w/index.php?title=3DTide&am=
p;action=3Dedit&amp;section=3D4" title=3D"Edit section: Phase and amplit=
ude">edit</a>]</span> <span class=3D"mw-headline">Phase and amplitude</s=
pan></h3>
<div class=3D"thumb tright">
<div class=3D"thumbinner" style=3D"width:352px;"><a href=3D"/wiki/File:M=
2_tidal_constituent.jpg" class=3D"image" title=3D"Fig. 4: The M2 tidal c=
onstituent. Amplitude is indicated by color, and the white lines are cot=
idal differing by 1 hour. The curved arcs around the amphidromic points =
show the direction of the tides, each indicating a synchronized 6 hour p=
eriod.[9]"><img alt=3D"" src=3D"http://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/co=
mmons/thumb/5/5e/M2_tidal_constituent.jpg/350px-M2_tidal_constituent.jpg=
" width=3D"350" height=3D"225" class=3D"thumbimage" /></a>
<div class=3D"thumbcaption">
<div class=3D"magnify"><a href=3D"/wiki/File:M2_tidal_constituent.jpg" c=
lass=3D"internal" title=3D"Enlarge"><img src=3D"/skins-1.5/common/images=
/magnify-clip.png" width=3D"15" height=3D"11" alt=3D"" /></a></div>
<i>Fig. 4:</i> The <i>M</i><sub>2</sub> tidal constituent. Amplitude is =
indicated by color, and the white lines are cotidal differing by 1 hour.=
 The curved arcs around the <a href=3D"/wiki/Amphidromic_point" title=3D=
"Amphidromic point">amphidromic points</a> show the direction of the tid=
es, each indicating a synchronized 6 hour period.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-8" =
class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"#cite_note-8"><span>[</span>9<span>]</spa=
n></a></sup></div>
</div>
</div>
<p>Because the <i>M</i><sub>2</sub> tidal constituent dominates in most =
locations, the stage or <i>phase</i> of a tide, denoted by the time in h=
ours after high water, is a useful concept. It is also measured in degre=
es, with 360=C2=B0 per tidal cycle. Lines of constant tidal phase are ca=
lled <i>cotidal lines</i>. High water is reached simultaneously along th=
e cotidal lines extending from the coast out into the ocean, and cotidal=
 lines (and hence tidal phases) advance along the coast.<sup id=3D"cite_=
ref-9" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"#cite_note-9"><span>[</span>10<spa=
n>]</span></a></sup></p>
<p>For an ocean in the shape of a circular basin enclosed by a coastline=
, the <i>cotidal lines</i> point radially inward and must eventually mee=
t at a common point, the <a href=3D"/wiki/Amphidromic_point" title=3D"Am=
phidromic point">amphidromic point</a>. The amphidromic point is at once=
 cotidal with high and low waters, which is satisfied by <i>zero</i> tid=
al motion. (The rare exception occurs when the tide circles around an is=
land, as it does around <a href=3D"/wiki/New_Zealand" title=3D"New Zeala=
nd">New Zealand</a> and <a href=3D"/wiki/Madagascar" title=3D"Madagascar=
">Madagascar</a>.) Indeed tidal motion generally lessens moving away fro=
m the continental coasts, so that crossing the cotidal lines are contour=
s of constant <i>amplitude</i> (half of the distance between high and lo=
w water) which decrease to zero at the amphidromic point. For a 12 hour =
<i>semidiurnal tide</i> the amphidromic point behaves roughly like the c=
enter of a clock face, with the hour hand pointing in the direction of t=
he high water cotidal line, which is directly opposite the low water cot=
idal line. High water rotates about the amphidromic point once every 12 =
hours in the direction of rising cotidal lines, and away from ebbing cot=
idal lines. This rotation is generally clockwise in the southern hemisph=
ere and counterclockwise in the northern hemisphere, and is caused by th=
e <a href=3D"/wiki/Coriolis_effect" title=3D"Coriolis effect">Coriolis e=
ffect</a>. The difference of cotidal phase from the phase of a reference=
 tide is the <i>epoch</i>.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-10" class=3D"reference"><a=
 href=3D"#cite_note-10"><span>[</span>11<span>]</span></a></sup></p>
<p>The shape of the shoreline and the ocean floor change the way that ti=
des propagate, so there is no simple, general rule for predicting the ti=
me of high water from the position of the Moon in the sky. Coastal chara=
cteristics such as underwater topography and coastline shape mean that i=
ndividual location characteristics need to be taken into consideration w=
hen forecasting tides; high water time and height may differ from that s=
uggested by a model such as the one above due to the effects of coastal =
morphology on tidal flow. However, for a given location the relationship=
 between moon's <a href=3D"/wiki/Altitude_(astronomy)" title=3D"Altitude=
 (astronomy)" class=3D"mw-redirect">altitude</a> and the time of high or=
 low tide (the <a href=3D"/wiki/Lunitidal_interval" title=3D"Lunitidal i=
nterval">lunitidal interval</a>) is relatively constant and predictable,=
 as is the time of high or low tide relative to other points on the same=
 coast. For example, the high tide at Norfolk, VA predictably occurs app=
roximately two and a half hours ahead of the time that the moon passes d=
irectly overhead. Because the cotidal lines are circulating counterclock=
wise around the amphidromic point in the North Atlantic, the high tide p=
asses New York harbor approximately an hour ahead of Norfolk. South of C=
ape Hatteras the interaction of tidal forces is more complex, and cannot=
 be predicted reliably based on passage of the North Atlantic cotidal li=
nes.</p>
<p><a name=3D"Physics" id=3D"Physics"></a></p>
<h2><span class=3D"editsection">[<a href=3D"/w/index.php?title=3DTide&am=
p;action=3Dedit&amp;section=3D5" title=3D"Edit section: Physics">edit</a=
>]</span> <span class=3D"mw-headline">Physics</span></h2>
<div class=3D"rellink boilerplate seealso">See also: <a href=3D"/wiki/Ti=
dal_force" title=3D"Tidal force">Tidal force</a></div>
<div class=3D"thumb tleft">
<div class=3D"thumbinner" style=3D"width:377px;"><a href=3D"/wiki/File:L=
unar-Phase-Diagram.png" class=3D"image" title=3D"Fig. 5: The Earth and M=
oon, looking at the North Pole"><img alt=3D"" src=3D"http://upload.wikim=
edia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/72/Lunar-Phase-Diagram.png/375px-Luna=
r-Phase-Diagram.png" width=3D"375" height=3D"375" class=3D"thumbimage" /=
></a>
<div class=3D"thumbcaption">
<div class=3D"magnify"><a href=3D"/wiki/File:Lunar-Phase-Diagram.png" cl=
ass=3D"internal" title=3D"Enlarge"><img src=3D"/skins-1.5/common/images/=
magnify-clip.png" width=3D"15" height=3D"11" alt=3D"" /></a></div>
<i>Fig. 5:</i> The Earth and Moon, looking at the <a href=3D"/wiki/North=
_Pole" title=3D"North Pole">North Pole</a></div>
</div>
</div>
<p><a href=3D"/wiki/Isaac_Newton" title=3D"Isaac Newton">Isaac Newton</a=
> laid the foundations for the mathematical explanation of tides in the =
<i><a href=3D"/wiki/Philosophiae_Naturalis_Principia_Mathematica" title=3D=
"Philosophiae Naturalis Principia Mathematica" class=3D"mw-redirect">Phi=
losophiae Naturalis Principia Mathematica</a></i> (1687). In 1740, the <=
a href=3D"/wiki/Acad%C3%A9mie_Royale_des_Sciences" title=3D"Acad=C3=A9mi=
e Royale des Sciences" class=3D"mw-redirect">Acad=C3=A9mie Royale des Sc=
iences</a> in Paris offered a prize for the best theoretical essay on ti=
des. <a href=3D"/wiki/Daniel_Bernoulli" title=3D"Daniel Bernoulli">Danie=
l Bernoulli</a>, <a href=3D"/w/index.php?title=3DAntoine_Cavalleri&amp;a=
ction=3Dedit&amp;redlink=3D1" class=3D"new" title=3D"Antoine Cavalleri (=
page does not exist)">Antoine Cavalleri</a>, <a href=3D"/wiki/Leonhard_E=
uler" title=3D"Leonhard Euler">Leonhard Euler</a>, and <a href=3D"/wiki/=
Colin_Maclaurin" title=3D"Colin Maclaurin">Colin Maclaurin</a> shared th=
e prize. Maclaurin used Newton=E2=80=99s theory to show that a smooth sp=
here covered by a sufficiently deep ocean under the tidal force of a sin=
gle deforming body is a <a href=3D"/wiki/Prolate" title=3D"Prolate" clas=
s=3D"mw-redirect">prolate</a> spheroid with major axis directed toward t=
he deforming body. Maclaurin was the first to write about the Earth's <a=
 href=3D"/wiki/Coriolis_effect" title=3D"Coriolis effect">rotational eff=
ects</a> on motion. Euler realized that the <i>horizontal</i> component =
of the tidal force (more than the vertical) drives the tide.</p>
<p>In 1744 <a href=3D"/wiki/Jean_le_Rond_d%27Alembert" title=3D"Jean le =
Rond d'Alembert">Jean le Rond d'Alembert</a> studied tidal equations for=
 the atmosphere which did not include rotation. The first major theoreti=
cal formulation for water tides was made by <a href=3D"/wiki/Pierre-Simo=
n_Laplace" title=3D"Pierre-Simon Laplace">Pierre-Simon Laplace</a>, who =
formulated a system of partial differential equations relating the horiz=
ontal flow to the surface height of the ocean. The <a href=3D"/wiki/Theo=
ry_of_tides#Laplace.27s_tidal_equations" title=3D"Theory of tides">Lapla=
ce tidal equations</a> are still in use today. <a href=3D"/wiki/William_=
Thomson,_1st_Baron_Kelvin" title=3D"William Thomson, 1st Baron Kelvin">W=
illiam Thomson, 1st Baron Kelvin</a>, rewrote Laplace's equations in ter=
ms of <a href=3D"/wiki/Vorticity" title=3D"Vorticity">vorticity</a> whic=
h allowed for solutions describing tidally driven coastally trapped wave=
s, which are known as <a href=3D"/wiki/Kelvin_wave" title=3D"Kelvin wave=
">Kelvin waves</a>.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-tidhist_11-0" class=3D"reference"=
><a href=3D"#cite_note-tidhist-11"><span>[</span>12<span>]</span></a></s=
up></p>
<p>Regarding the Earth=E2=80=93Moon system by itself (excluding the Sun =
for the moment) it is known that unless the spin axes of both partners a=
re aligned and perpendicular to the orbital plane, oscillations are exci=
ted and these tidal deformations contribute somewhat to the tidal dissip=
ation.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-12" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"#cite_note-=
12"><span>[</span>13<span>]</span></a></sup> This lack of alignment is t=
he case for the Earth=E2=80=93Moon system. Thus, besides the tidal bulge=
s, opposite to each other and comparable in size, that are associated wi=
th the so called equilibrium tide,<sup id=3D"cite_ref-Boon_13-0" class=3D=
"reference"><a href=3D"#cite_note-Boon-13"><span>[</span>14<span>]</span=
></a></sup> additionally, a set of surface oscillations commonly known a=
s the dynamical tide, characterized by a wide variety of harmonic freque=
ncies, is established.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-Toledano_14-0" class=3D"refere=
nce"><a href=3D"#cite_note-Toledano-14"><span>[</span>15<span>]</span></=
a></sup><sup id=3D"cite_ref-Lamb_15-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"#c=
ite_note-Lamb-15"><span>[</span>16<span>]</span></a></sup><sup id=3D"cit=
e_ref-Americana_16-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"#cite_note-American=
a-16"><span>[</span>17<span>]</span></a></sup></p>
<p><a name=3D"Forces" id=3D"Forces"></a></p>
<h3><span class=3D"editsection">[<a href=3D"/w/index.php?title=3DTide&am=
p;action=3Dedit&amp;section=3D6" title=3D"Edit section: Forces">edit</a>=
]</span> <span class=3D"mw-headline">Forces</span></h3>
<p>The <a href=3D"/wiki/Tidal_force" title=3D"Tidal force">tidal force</=
a> produced by a massive object (Moon, hereafter) on a small particle lo=
cated on or in an extensive body (Earth, hereafter) is the vector differ=
ence between the gravitational force exerted by the Moon on the particle=
, and the gravitational force that would be exerted on the particle if i=
t were located at the center of mass of the Earth. Thus, the tidal force=
 depends not on the strength of the gravitational field of the Moon, but=
 on its gradient (which falls off approximately as the inverse cube of t=
he distance to the originating gravitational body; see<a href=3D"http://=
science.nasa.gov/headlines/y2000/ast04may_1m.htm" class=3D"external text=
" title=3D"http://science.nasa.gov/headlines/y2000/ast04may_1m.htm" rel=3D=
"nofollow">NASA</a>).<sup id=3D"cite_ref-difference_17-0" class=3D"refer=
ence"><a href=3D"#cite_note-difference-17"><span>[</span>18<span>]</span=
></a></sup> The <i><a href=3D"/wiki/Gravitational_force" title=3D"Gravit=
ational force" class=3D"mw-redirect">gravitational force</a></i> exerted=
 on the Earth by the Sun is on average 179 times stronger than that exer=
ted on the Earth by the Moon, but because the Sun is on average 389 time=
s farther from the Earth, the gradient of its field is weaker. The tidal=
 force produced by the Sun is therefore only 46% as large as that produc=
ed by the Moon. (According to <a href=3D"http://imagine.gsfc.nasa.gov/do=
cs/ask_astro/answers/961029b.html" class=3D"external text" title=3D"http=
://imagine.gsfc.nasa.gov/docs/ask_astro/answers/961029b.html" rel=3D"nof=
ollow">NASA</a> the tidal force of the Moon is 2.21 times larger than th=
at of the Sun. The effect of the other planets is much, much smaller, wi=
th the largest being Venus at 0.000113 times that of the Sun.)</p>
<div class=3D"thumb tright">
<div class=3D"thumbinner" style=3D"width:182px;"><a href=3D"/wiki/File:F=
ield_tidal.png" class=3D"image" title=3D"Fig. 6: The Moon's gravity diff=
erential field at the surface of the Earth is known as the tide generati=
ng force. This is the primary mechanism that drives tidal action and exp=
lains two tidal equipotential bulges, accounting for two high waters per=
 day."><img alt=3D"" src=3D"http://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/common=
s/thumb/d/d8/Field_tidal.png/180px-Field_tidal.png" width=3D"180" height=
=3D"138" class=3D"thumbimage" /></a>
<div class=3D"thumbcaption">
<div class=3D"magnify"><a href=3D"/wiki/File:Field_tidal.png" class=3D"i=
nternal" title=3D"Enlarge"><img src=3D"/skins-1.5/common/images/magnify-=
clip.png" width=3D"15" height=3D"11" alt=3D"" /></a></div>
<i>Fig. 6:</i> The Moon's <a href=3D"/wiki/Gravity" title=3D"Gravity" cl=
ass=3D"mw-redirect">gravity</a> differential <a href=3D"/wiki/Vector_fie=
ld" title=3D"Vector field">field</a> at the surface of the Earth is know=
n as the <a href=3D"/wiki/Tidal_force" title=3D"Tidal force">tide genera=
ting force</a>. This is the primary mechanism that drives tidal action a=
nd explains two tidal equipotential bulges, accounting for two high wate=
rs per day.</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>Tidal forces can also be analyzed in the following way: each point of=
 the Earth experiences the Moon's radially decreasing gravity differentl=
y; they are subject to the <i><a href=3D"/wiki/Tidal_force" title=3D"Tid=
al force">tidal force</a></i>s of Figure 6, which dominate. Finally, mos=
t importantly, only the <i>horizontal</i> components of the tidal forces=
 actually contribute tidal acceleration to the water particles since the=
re is small resistance. The actual tidal force on a particle is only abo=
ut a ten millionth of the force caused by the Earth's gravity.</p>
<p>The ocean's surface is closely approximated by an equipotential surfa=
ce, (ignoring ocean currents) which is commonly referred to as the <a hr=
ef=3D"/wiki/Geoid" title=3D"Geoid">geoid</a>. Since the gravitational fo=
rce is equal to the <a href=3D"/wiki/Gradient" title=3D"Gradient">gradie=
nt</a> of the potential, there are no tangential forces on such a surfac=
e, and the ocean surface is thus in gravitational equilibrium. Now consi=
der the effect of external, massive bodies such as the Moon and Sun. The=
se bodies have strong gravitational fields that diminish with distance i=
n space and which act to alter the shape of an equipotential surface on =
the Earth. Gravitational forces follow an <a href=3D"/wiki/Inverse-squar=
e_law" title=3D"Inverse-square law">inverse-square law</a> (force is inv=
ersely proportional to the <a href=3D"/wiki/Square_(algebra)" title=3D"S=
quare (algebra)">square</a> of the distance), but tidal forces are inver=
sely proportional to the <a href=3D"/wiki/Cube_(arithmetic)" title=3D"Cu=
be (arithmetic)" class=3D"mw-redirect">cube</a> of the distance. The oce=
an surface moves to adjust to changing tidal equipotential, tending to r=
ise when the tidal potential is high, the part of the Earth nearest the =
Moon, and the farthest part. When the tidal equipotential changes, the o=
cean surface is no longer aligned with it, so that the apparent directio=
n of the vertical shifts. The surface then experiences a down slope, in =
the direction that the equipotential has risen.</p>
<p><a name=3D"Laplace_tidal_equation" id=3D"Laplace_tidal_equation"></a>=
</p>
<h3><span class=3D"editsection">[<a href=3D"/w/index.php?title=3DTide&am=
p;action=3Dedit&amp;section=3D7" title=3D"Edit section: Laplace tidal eq=
uation">edit</a>]</span> <span class=3D"mw-headline">Laplace tidal equat=
ion</span></h3>
<p>The depth of the oceans is much smaller than their horizontal extent;=
 thus, the response to tidal forcing can be <a href=3D"/wiki/Model_(abst=
ract)" title=3D"Model (abstract)" class=3D"mw-redirect">modelled</a> usi=
ng the <a href=3D"/wiki/Theory_of_tides#Laplace.27s_tidal_equations" tit=
le=3D"Theory of tides">Laplace tidal equations</a> which incorporate the=
 following features:</p>
<ol>
<li>The vertical (or radial) velocity is negligible, and there is no ver=
tical <a href=3D"/wiki/Wind_shear" title=3D"Wind shear">shear</a>=E2=80=94=
this is a sheet flow.</li>
<li>The forcing is only horizontal (tangential).</li>
<li>The <a href=3D"/wiki/Coriolis_effect" title=3D"Coriolis effect">Cori=
olis effect</a> appears as a fictitious lateral forcing proportional to =
velocity.</li>
<li>The rate of change of the surface height is proportional to the nega=
tive divergence of velocity multiplied by the depth. As the horizontal v=
elocity stretches or compresses the ocean as a sheet, the volume thins o=
r thickens, respectively.</li>
</ol>
<p>The boundary conditions dictate no flow across the coastline and free=
 slip at the bottom. The Coriolis effect steers waves to the right in th=
e northern hemisphere and to the left in the southern allowing coastally=
 trapped waves. Finally, a dissipation term can be added which is an ana=
log to viscosity.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-18" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"=
#cite_note-18"><span>[</span>19<span>]</span></a></sup></p>
<p><a name=3D"Amplitude_and_cycle_time" id=3D"Amplitude_and_cycle_time">=
</a></p>
<h3><span class=3D"editsection">[<a href=3D"/w/index.php?title=3DTide&am=
p;action=3Dedit&amp;section=3D8" title=3D"Edit section: Amplitude and cy=
cle time">edit</a>]</span> <span class=3D"mw-headline">Amplitude and cyc=
le time</span></h3>
<p>The theoretical amplitude of oceanic tides caused by the Moon is abou=
t 54&#160;cm at the highest point, which corresponds to the amplitude th=
at would be reached if the ocean possessed a uniform depth, there were n=
o landmasses, and the Earth were not rotating. The Sun similarly causes =
tides, of which the theoretical amplitude is about 25&#160;cm (46% of th=
at of the Moon) with a cycle time of 12 hours. At spring tide the two ef=
fects add to each other to a theoretical level of 79&#160;cm, while at n=
eap tide the theoretical level is reduced to 29&#160;cm. Since the orbit=
s of the Earth about the Sun, and the Moon about the Earth, are elliptic=
al, the amplitudes of the tides change somewhat as a result of the varyi=
ng Earth=E2=80=93Sun and Earth=E2=80=93Moon distances. This causes a var=
iation in the tidal force and theoretical amplitude of about =C2=B118% f=
or the Moon and =C2=B15% for the Sun. If both the Sun and Moon were at t=
heir closest positions and aligned at new moon, the theoretical amplitud=
e would reach 93&#160;cm.</p>
<p>Real amplitudes differ considerably, not only because of variations i=
n ocean depth, and the obstacles to flow caused by the continents, but a=
lso because the natural period of wave propagation across the ocean is o=
f the same order of magnitude as the rotation period: if there were no l=
and masses, it would take about 30 hours for a long wavelength ocean sur=
face wave to propagate along the equator halfway around the Earth (by co=
mparison, the natural period of the Earth's lithosphere is about 57 minu=
tes).</p>
<p><a name=3D"Dissipation" id=3D"Dissipation"></a></p>
<h3><span class=3D"editsection">[<a href=3D"/w/index.php?title=3DTide&am=
p;action=3Dedit&amp;section=3D9" title=3D"Edit section: Dissipation">edi=
t</a>]</span> <span class=3D"mw-headline">Dissipation</span></h3>
<div class=3D"rellink boilerplate seealso">See also: <a href=3D"/wiki/Ti=
dal_acceleration" title=3D"Tidal acceleration">Tidal acceleration</a></d=
iv>
<p>The tidal oscillations of the Earth introduce dissipation, at an <a h=
ref=3D"/wiki/Average" title=3D"Average">average</a> rate of about 3.75 <=
a href=3D"/wiki/Terawatt" title=3D"Terawatt" class=3D"mw-redirect">teraw=
att</a>.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-Munk1998_19-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D=
"#cite_note-Munk1998-19"><span>[</span>20<span>]</span></a></sup> About =
98% of this dissipation is by the tidal movement in the seas and oceans.=
<sup id=3D"cite_ref-Ray1996_20-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"#cite_n=
ote-Ray1996-20"><span>[</span>21<span>]</span></a></sup> The dissipation=
 arises as the basin-scale tidal flow drives smaller-scale flows which e=
xperience turbulent dissipation. This tidal drag gives rise to a torque =
on the Moon that results in the gradual transfer of angular momentum to =
its orbit, and a gradual increase in the Earth=E2=80=93Moon separation. =
As a result of the equal and opposite torque on the Earth, the rotationa=
l velocity of the Earth is correspondingly slowed. Thus, over geologic t=
ime, the Moon recedes from the Earth, at about 3.8&#160;cm/year, and the=
 length of the terrestrial day increases.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-21" class=3D=
"reference"><a href=3D"#cite_note-21"><span>[</span>22<span>]</span></a>=
</sup> The length of a day has increased by about 2 hours in the last 60=
0 million years (see <a href=3D"/wiki/Tidal_acceleration" title=3D"Tidal=
 acceleration">Tidal acceleration</a>). Assuming (as a crude approximati=
on) that the rate of deceleration has been constant, this would imply th=
at 70 million years ago, the day was on the order of 1% shorter and ther=
e would have been about 4 more days per year.</p>
<p><a name=3D"Observation_and_prediction" id=3D"Observation_and_predicti=
on"></a></p>
<h2><span class=3D"editsection">[<a href=3D"/w/index.php?title=3DTide&am=
p;action=3Dedit&amp;section=3D10" title=3D"Edit section: Observation and=
 prediction">edit</a>]</span> <span class=3D"mw-headline">Observation an=
d prediction</span></h2>
<p><a name=3D"History" id=3D"History"></a></p>
<h3><span class=3D"editsection">[<a href=3D"/w/index.php?title=3DTide&am=
p;action=3Dedit&amp;section=3D11" title=3D"Edit section: History">edit</=
a>]</span> <span class=3D"mw-headline">History</span></h3>
<p>From ancient times, tides have been observed and discussed with incre=
asing sophistication, first noting the daily recurrence, then its relati=
onship to the Sun and Moon. <a href=3D"/wiki/Pytheas" title=3D"Pytheas">=
Pytheas</a> travelled to the <a href=3D"/wiki/British_Isles" title=3D"Br=
itish Isles">British Isles</a> about 325 BC and seems to be the first to=
 have related spring tides to the phase of the Moon.</p>
<p>In the 2nd century BC, the <a href=3D"/wiki/Babylonian_astronomy" tit=
le=3D"Babylonian astronomy">Babylonian astronomer</a>, <a href=3D"/wiki/=
Seleucus_of_Seleucia" title=3D"Seleucus of Seleucia">Seleucus of Seleuci=
a</a>, correctly described the phenomenon of tides in order to support h=
is <a href=3D"/wiki/Heliocentrism" title=3D"Heliocentrism">heliocentric<=
/a> theory.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-22" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"#cite_=
note-22"><span>[</span>23<span>]</span></a></sup> He correctly theorized=
 that tides were caused by the <a href=3D"/wiki/Moon" title=3D"Moon">Moo=
n</a>, although he believed that the interaction was mediated by the <a =
href=3D"/wiki/Pneuma" title=3D"Pneuma">pneuma</a>. He noted that the tid=
es varied in time and strength in different parts of the world. Accordin=
g to <a href=3D"/wiki/Strabo" title=3D"Strabo">Strabo</a> (1.1.9), Seleu=
cus was the first to state that the tides are due to the attraction of t=
he Moon, and that the height of the tides depends on the Moon's position=
 relative to the Sun.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-23" class=3D"reference"><a href=
=3D"#cite_note-23"><span>[</span>24<span>]</span></a></sup></p>
<p>The <a href=3D"/wiki/Natural_History_(Pliny)" title=3D"Natural Histor=
y (Pliny)"><i>Naturalis Historia</i></a> of <a href=3D"/wiki/Pliny_the_E=
lder" title=3D"Pliny the Elder">Pliny the Elder</a> collates many observ=
ations of detail: the spring tides being a few days after (or before) ne=
w and full moon, and that the spring tides around the time of the equino=
xes were the highest, though there were also many relationships now rega=
rded as fanciful. In his <i>Geography</i>, <a href=3D"/wiki/Strabo" titl=
e=3D"Strabo">Strabo</a> described tides in the <a href=3D"/wiki/Persian_=
Gulf" title=3D"Persian Gulf">Persian Gulf</a> having their greatest rang=
e when the Moon was furthest from the plane of the equator. All this des=
pite the relatively feeble tides in the <a href=3D"/wiki/Mediterranean" =
title=3D"Mediterranean" class=3D"mw-redirect">Mediterranean</a> basin, t=
hough there are strong currents through the <a href=3D"/wiki/Strait_of_M=
essina" title=3D"Strait of Messina">Strait of Messina</a> and between <a=
 href=3D"/wiki/Greece" title=3D"Greece">Greece</a> and the island of <a =
href=3D"/wiki/Euboea" title=3D"Euboea">Euboea</a> through the <a href=3D=
"/wiki/Euripus" title=3D"Euripus" class=3D"mw-redirect">Euripus</a> that=
 puzzled <a href=3D"/wiki/Aristotle" title=3D"Aristotle">Aristotle</a>. =
<a href=3D"/wiki/Philostratus" title=3D"Philostratus">Philostratus</a> d=
iscussed tides in Book Five of The Life of <a href=3D"/wiki/Apollonius_o=
f_Tyana" title=3D"Apollonius of Tyana">Apollonius of Tyana</a>. Philostr=
atus mentions the moon, but attributes tides to "spirits". In Europe aro=
und 730 AD, the Venerable <a href=3D"/wiki/Bede" title=3D"Bede">Bede</a>=
 described how the rise of tide on one coast of the British Isles coinci=
ded with the fall on the other and described the progression in times of=
 the same high water along the Northumbrian coast.</p>
<p>In the 9th century, the <a href=3D"/wiki/Islamic_geography" title=3D"=
Islamic geography" class=3D"mw-redirect">Arabian earth-scientist</a>, <a=
 href=3D"/wiki/Al-Kindi" title=3D"Al-Kindi">Al-Kindi</a> (Alkindus), wro=
te a treatise entitled <i>Risala fi l-Illa al-Failali l-Madd wa l-Fazr</=
i> (<i>Treatise on the Efficient Cause of the Flow and Ebb</i>), in whic=
h he presents an argument on tides which "depends on the changes which t=
ake place in bodies owing to the rise and fall of <a href=3D"/wiki/Tempe=
rature" title=3D"Temperature">temperature</a>."<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FSTC_=
24-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"#cite_note-FSTC-24"><span>[</span>2=
5<span>]</span></a></sup> He describes a clear and precise <a href=3D"/w=
iki/Laboratory" title=3D"Laboratory">laboratory</a> <a href=3D"/wiki/Exp=
eriment" title=3D"Experiment">experiment</a> in order to prove his argum=
ent.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-Plinio-17_25-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"#=
cite_note-Plinio-17-25"><span>[</span>26<span>]</span></a></sup></p>
<p>The first tide table in <a href=3D"/wiki/China" title=3D"China">China=
</a> was recorded in 1056 AD primarily for the benefit of visitors wishi=
ng to see the famous <a href=3D"/wiki/Tidal_bore" title=3D"Tidal bore">t=
idal bore</a> in the <a href=3D"/wiki/Qiantang_River" title=3D"Qiantang =
River">Qiantang River</a>. The first known tide table is thought to be t=
hat of John, Abbott of Wallingford (d. 1213), based on high water occurr=
ing 48 minutes later each day, and three hours later upriver at <a href=3D=
"/wiki/London" title=3D"London">London</a> than at the mouth of the <a h=
ref=3D"/wiki/Thames" title=3D"Thames" class=3D"mw-redirect">Thames</a>.<=
/p>
<p><a href=3D"/wiki/William_Thomson,_1st_Baron_Kelvin" title=3D"William =
Thomson, 1st Baron Kelvin">William Thomson (Lord Kelvin)</a> led the fir=
st systematic <a href=3D"/wiki/Harmonic_analysis" title=3D"Harmonic anal=
ysis">harmonic analysis</a> of tidal records starting in 1867. The main =
result was the building of a tide-predicting machine using a system of p=
ulleys to add together six harmonic functions of time. It was "programme=
d" by resetting gears and chains to adjust phasing and amplitudes. Simil=
ar machines were used until the 1960s.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-26" class=3D"r=
eference"><a href=3D"#cite_note-26"><span>[</span>27<span>]</span></a></=
sup></p>
<p>The first known sea-level record of an entire spring=E2=80=93neap cyc=
le was made in 1831 on the Navy Dock in the Thames Estuary, and many lar=
ge ports had automatic tide gage stations by 1850.</p>
<p>William Whewell first mapped co-tidal lines ending with a nearly glob=
al chart in 1836. In order to make these maps consistent, he hypothesize=
d the existence of amphidromes where co-tidal lines meet in the mid-ocea=
n. These points of no tide were confirmed by measurement in 1840 by Capt=
ain Hewett, RN, from careful soundings in the North Sea.<sup id=3D"cite_=
ref-tidhist_11-1" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"#cite_note-tidhist-11">=
<span>[</span>12<span>]</span></a></sup></p>
<p><a name=3D"Timing" id=3D"Timing"></a></p>
<h3><span class=3D"editsection">[<a href=3D"/w/index.php?title=3DTide&am=
p;action=3Dedit&amp;section=3D12" title=3D"Edit section: Timing">edit</a=
>]</span> <span class=3D"mw-headline">Timing</span></h3>
<div class=3D"thumb tleft">
<div class=3D"thumbinner" style=3D"width:402px;"><a href=3D"/wiki/File:T=
ide24_12_mix.gif" class=3D"image" title=3D"Fig. 7: The same tidal forcin=
g has different results depending on many factors, including coast orien=
tation, continental shelf margin, water body dimensions."><img alt=3D"" =
src=3D"http://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/f/fa/Tide24_12_mix.gif" =
width=3D"400" height=3D"215" class=3D"thumbimage" /></a>
<div class=3D"thumbcaption">
<div class=3D"magnify"><a href=3D"/wiki/File:Tide24_12_mix.gif" class=3D=
"internal" title=3D"Enlarge"><img src=3D"/skins-1.5/common/images/magnif=
y-clip.png" width=3D"15" height=3D"11" alt=3D"" /></a></div>
<i>Fig. 7:</i> The same tidal forcing has different results depending on=
 many factors, including coast orientation, continental shelf margin, wa=
ter body dimensions.</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>In most places there is a delay between the phases of the Moon and th=
e effect on the tide. Springs and neaps in the <a href=3D"/wiki/North_Se=
a" title=3D"North Sea">North Sea</a>, for example, are two days behind t=
he new/full Moon and first/third quarter. This is called the <i>age of t=
he tide</i>.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-27" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"#cite=
_note-27"><span>[</span>28<span>]</span></a></sup></p>
<p>The exact time and height of the tide at a particular <a href=3D"/wik=
i/Coast" title=3D"Coast">coastal</a> point is also greatly influenced by=
 the local <a href=3D"/wiki/Bathymetry" title=3D"Bathymetry">bathymetry<=
/a>. There are some extreme cases: the <a href=3D"/wiki/Bay_of_Fundy" ti=
tle=3D"Bay of Fundy">Bay of Fundy</a>, on the east coast of <a href=3D"/=
wiki/Canada" title=3D"Canada">Canada</a>, features the largest well-docu=
mented tidal ranges in the world, 16 meters (53 ft), because of the shap=
e of the bay.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-28" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"#cit=
e_note-28"><span>[</span>29<span>]</span></a></sup> <a href=3D"/wiki/Ung=
ava_Bay" title=3D"Ungava Bay">Ungava Bay</a> in northern <a href=3D"/wik=
i/Quebec" title=3D"Quebec">Quebec</a>, is believed by some experts to ha=
ve higher tidal ranges than the Bay of Fundy, but it is free of <a href=3D=
"/wiki/Pack_ice" title=3D"Pack ice" class=3D"mw-redirect">pack ice</a> f=
or only about four months every year, whereas the Bay of Fundy rarely fr=
eezes.</p>
<p><a href=3D"/wiki/Southampton" title=3D"Southampton">Southampton</a> i=
n the <a href=3D"/wiki/United_Kingdom" title=3D"United Kingdom">United K=
ingdom</a> has a double high water caused by the interaction between the=
 different tidal harmonics within the region. This is contrary to the po=
pular belief that the flow of water around the <a href=3D"/wiki/Isle_of_=
Wight" title=3D"Isle of Wight">Isle of Wight</a> creates two high waters=
. The Isle of Wight is important, however, since it is responsible for t=
he 'Young Flood Stand', which describes the pause of the incoming tide a=
bout three hours after low water.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-29" class=3D"refere=
nce"><a href=3D"#cite_note-29"><span>[</span>30<span>]</span></a></sup><=
/p>
<p>Because the oscillation modes of the <a href=3D"/wiki/Mediterranean_S=
ea" title=3D"Mediterranean Sea">Mediterranean Sea</a> and the <a href=3D=
"/wiki/Baltic_Sea" title=3D"Baltic Sea">Baltic Sea</a> do not coincide w=
ith any significant astronomical forcing period, the largest tides are c=
lose to their narrow connections with the Atlantic Ocean. Extremely smal=
l tides also occur for the same reason in the <a href=3D"/wiki/Gulf_of_M=
exico" title=3D"Gulf of Mexico">Gulf of Mexico</a> and <a href=3D"/wiki/=
Sea_of_Japan" title=3D"Sea of Japan">Sea of Japan</a>. On the southern c=
oast of <a href=3D"/wiki/Australia" title=3D"Australia">Australia</a>, b=
ecause the coast is mainly straight (partly because of the tiny quantiti=
es of <a href=3D"/wiki/Runoff_(water)" title=3D"Runoff (water)" class=3D=
"mw-redirect">runoff</a> flowing from rivers), tidal ranges are equally =
small.</p>
<p><a name=3D"Analysis" id=3D"Analysis"></a></p>
<h3><span class=3D"editsection">[<a href=3D"/w/index.php?title=3DTide&am=
p;action=3Dedit&amp;section=3D13" title=3D"Edit section: Analysis">edit<=
/a>]</span> <span class=3D"mw-headline">Analysis</span></h3>
<p>It was <a href=3D"/wiki/Isaac_Newton" title=3D"Isaac Newton">Isaac Ne=
wton</a>'s universal theory of gravitation that first enabled an explana=
tion of why there were <i>two</i> tides a day, not one, and, via calcula=
tion of the forces, offered hope of detailed understanding. Although it =
may seem that tides could be predicted via a sufficiently detailed knowl=
edge of the astronomical forcing terms, the actual tide at a given locat=
ion is determined by the response of the oceans to the astronomical forc=
es accumulated over a period of many days. To calculate this response re=
quires a detailed knowledge of the shape of all the ocean basins=E2=80=94=
their bathymetry and coastline shape.</p>
<p>Instead of a direct calculation, the procedure for analysing tides is=
 pragmatic: At each place of interest, the tide heights are measured for=
 at least a lunar cycle. The tide heights are compared to the known freq=
uencies of the astronomical tide-raising forces. The behaviour of the ti=
de heights is expected to follow the behaviour of the tide force, with t=
he amplitude and delays of those responses remaining constant. Because a=
stronomical frequencies and phases can be calculated with certainty, the=
 tide height at other times can be predicted once the response to the as=
tronomical states has been found.</p>
<p>The main patterns in the tides are</p>
<ul>
<li>the twice-daily combined lunar and solar tide, caused by the rotatio=
n of the Earth,</li>
<li>the difference between the first and second tide of a day, due to th=
e Moon or Sun being north or south of the equator,</li>
<li>the spring=E2=80=93neap cycle in amplitude (related to the relative =
positions of the Moon and Sun), and</li>
<li>the adjustment of tide heights due to the varying distances to the M=
oon and Sun.</li>
</ul>
<p>The <i>Highest Astronomical Tide</i> is the perigean spring tide when=
 both the Sun and the Moon are closest to the Earth.</p>
<p>When confronted by a periodically varying function, the standard appr=
oach is to employ <a href=3D"/wiki/Fourier_series" title=3D"Fourier seri=
es">Fourier series</a>, a form of orthogonal analysis that uses <a href=3D=
"/wiki/Sinusoid" title=3D"Sinusoid" class=3D"mw-redirect">sinusoidal</a>=
 functions as a <i>basis</i> set, having frequencies that are zero, one,=
 two, three, etc. times the frequency of a particular fundamental cycle.=
 These multiples are called <i>harmonics</i> of the fundamental frequenc=
y, and the process is termed <a href=3D"/wiki/Harmonic_analysis" title=3D=
"Harmonic analysis">harmonic analysis</a>. If the basis set of sinusoida=
l functions are well-suited to the behaviour being modelled, relatively =
few harmonic terms need to be carried in the analysis. Fortunately orbit=
al paths are very nearly circular, so sinusoidal variations are suitable=
 for tides.</p>
<p>For the analysis of tide heights, the Fourier series approach is best=
 made more elaborate. While the theorem remains true and the tidal heigh=
t could be analysed in terms of a single frequency and its harmonics, a =
large number of significant terms would be required. A much more compact=
 decomposition for the tides involves a combination of sinusoids having =
more than one fundamental frequency. Specifically, the incommensurable p=
eriods of one revolution of the Earth (equivalently, of the Sun around t=
he Earth), and one orbit of the Moon about the Earth are used (for simpl=
icity in phrasing, this discussion is entirely geocentric, but is inform=
ed by the heliocentric model).</p>
<p>To represent both the lunar and solar influences using one frequency =
would require many harmonic terms, but allowing two incommensurable freq=
uencies requires only a few terms. That is, the sum of two sinusoids, on=
e at the Sun's frequency and the second at the Moon's frequency, require=
s those two terms only, but their representation as a Fourier series hav=
ing only one fundamental frequency and its (integer) multiples would req=
uire many terms. For tides then, although the process is still termed <i=
>harmonic analysis</i>, it is <i>not</i> limited to harmonics of a singl=
e frequency. To demonstrate this <sup id=3D"cite_ref-30" class=3D"refere=
nce"><a href=3D"#cite_note-30"><span>[</span>31<span>]</span></a></sup> =
offers a tidal height pattern converted into an <i>.mp3</i> sound file, =
and the rich sound is quite different from a pure tone. In other words, =
the harmonies are multiples of many fundamental frequencies, not just of=
 the one fundamental frequency of the simpler Fourier series approach.</=
p>
<p>The study of tide height by harmonic analysis was begun by Laplace, W=
illiam Thomson (Lord Kelvin), and <a href=3D"/wiki/George_Darwin" title=3D=
"George Darwin">George Darwin</a>. Their work was extended by <a href=3D=
"/wiki/Arthur_Thomas_Doodson" title=3D"Arthur Thomas Doodson">A.T. Doods=
on</a> who introduced the <i>Doodson Number</i> notation to organise the=
 hundreds of terms that result. This approach has been the international=
 standard ever since, and the complications arise as follows: the tide-r=
aising force is notionally given by sums of several terms. Each term is =
of the form</p>
<dl>
<dd><i>A</i>=C2=B7cos(<i>w</i>=C2=B7<i>t</i> + <i>p</i>)</dd>
</dl>
<p>where <i>A</i> is the amplitude, <i>w</i> is the angular frequency us=
ually given in degrees per hour corresponding to <i>t</i> measured in ho=
urs, and <i>p</i> is the phase offset with regard to the astronomical st=
ate at time <i>t</i> =3D 0&#160;. There is one term for the Moon and a s=
econd term for the Sun. The phase <i>p</i> of the first harmonic for the=
 Moon term is called the <a href=3D"/wiki/Lunitidal_interval" title=3D"L=
unitidal interval">lunitidal interval</a> or high water interval. If the=
 orbits were circular, that would be the end of the matter, but of cours=
e they are not. Accordingly, the value of <i>A</i> is not a constant but=
 also varying with time, slightly, about some average figure. Replace it=
 then by <i>A</i>(<i>t</i>)&#160;, but what functional form? It turns ou=
t that another sinusoid gives an excellent approximation for the changin=
g amplitude, similar to the cycles and epicycles of <a href=3D"/wiki/Pto=
lemy" title=3D"Ptolemy">Ptolemaic theory</a>. Accordingly,</p>
<dl>
<dd><i>A</i>(<i>t</i>) =3D <i>A</i>=C2=B7(1 + <i>A</i><sub>a</sub>=C2=B7=
cos(<i>w</i><sub>a</sub>=C2=B7<i>t</i> + <i>p</i><sub>a</sub>))&#160;,</=
dd>
</dl>
<p>which is to say an average value <i>A</i> with a sinusoidal variation=
 about it of magnitude <i>A</i><sub>a</sub>&#160;, with frequency <i>w</=
i><sub>a</sub> and phase <i>p</i><sub>a</sub>&#160;. Thus the simple ter=
m is now a compound term, the product of two cosine factors:</p>
<dl>
<dd><i>A</i>=C2=B7[1 + <i>A</i><sub>a</sub>=C2=B7cos(<i>w</i><sub>a</sub=
> + <i>p</i><sub>a</sub>)]=C2=B7cos(<i>w</i>=C2=B7<i>t</i> + <i>p</i>)</=
dd>
</dl>
<p>Now, given that for any <i>x</i> and <i>y</i></p>
<dl>
<dd>cos(<i>x</i>)=C2=B7cos(<i>y</i>) =3D =C2=BD=C2=B7cos( <i>x</i> + <i>=
y</i> ) + =C2=BD=C2=B7cos( <i>x</i>=E2=80=93<i>y</i> )&#160;,</dd>
</dl>
<p>it is clear that a compound term involving the product of two cosine =
terms each with their own frequency is the same as <i>three</i> simple c=
osine terms that are to be added, at the original frequency and also at =
the sum and difference of the two frequencies of the product term. (Thre=
e, not two terms, since the whole expression is (1 + cos(<i>x</i>))=C2=B7=
cos(<i>y</i>)&#160;.) Consider further that the tidal force on a locatio=
n depends also on whether the Moon (or the Sun) is above or below the pl=
ane of the equator, and that these attributes have their own periods als=
o incommensurable with a day and a month, and it is clear that many comb=
inations result. With a careful choice of the basic astronomical frequen=
cies, the Doodson Number annotates the particular additions and differen=
ces of them to form the frequency of each simple cosine term.</p>
<div class=3D"thumb tleft">
<div class=3D"thumbinner" style=3D"width:255px;"><a href=3D"/wiki/File:T=
idal_constituent_sum.gif" class=3D"image" title=3D"Fig. 8: Tidal predict=
ion summing constituent parts."><img alt=3D"" src=3D"http://upload.wikim=
edia.org/wikipedia/en/0/0e/Tidal_constituent_sum.gif" width=3D"253" heig=
ht=3D"322" class=3D"thumbimage" /></a>
<div class=3D"thumbcaption">
<div class=3D"magnify"><a href=3D"/wiki/File:Tidal_constituent_sum.gif" =
class=3D"internal" title=3D"Enlarge"><img src=3D"/skins-1.5/common/image=
s/magnify-clip.png" width=3D"15" height=3D"11" alt=3D"" /></a></div>
<i>Fig. 8:</i> Tidal prediction summing constituent parts.</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>Remember that astronomical tides do <i>not</i> include the effect of =
weather, and changes to local conditions (sandbank movement, dredging ha=
rbour mouths, etc.) away from those prevailing at the time of measuremen=
t can affect the timing and magnitude of the actual tide. Organisations =
quoting a "highest astronomical tide" for some location can exaggerate t=
he figure as a safety factor against uncertainties of analysis, extrapol=
ation from the nearest point of measurement, changes since the time of o=
bservation, possible ground subsidence, etc., to protect the organisatio=
n against blame should an engineering work be overtopped. If the size of=
 a "weather surge" is assessed by subtracting the astronomical tide from=
 the observed tide at the time, care is needed.</p>
<p>Careful Fourier <a href=3D"/wiki/Data_analysis" title=3D"Data analysi=
s">data analysis</a> over a nineteen-year period (the <i>National Tidal =
Datum Epoch</i> in the U.S.) uses frequencies called the <i>tidal harmon=
ic constituents</i>. Nineteen years is preferred because the relative po=
sitions of the Earth, Moon and Sun repeat almost exactly in the <a href=3D=
"/wiki/Metonic_cycle" title=3D"Metonic cycle">Metonic cycle</a> of 19&#1=
60;years, which is long enough to include the 18.613 year lunar nodal <a=
 href=3D"/wiki/Earth_tide#Tidal_constituents" title=3D"Earth tide">tidal=
 constituent</a>. This analysis can be done using only the knowledge of =
the <i>period</i> of forcing, but without detailed understanding of the =
mathematical derivation, which means that useful tidal tables have been =
constructed for centuries.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-31" class=3D"reference"><a=
 href=3D"#cite_note-31"><span>[</span>32<span>]</span></a></sup> The res=
ulting amplitudes and phases can then be used to predict the expected ti=
des. These are usually dominated by the constituents near 12 hours (the =
<i>semidiurnal</i> constituents), but there are major constituents near =
24&#160;hours (<i>diurnal</i>) as well. Longer term constituents are 14&=
#160;day or <i>fortnightly</i>, monthly, and semiannual. Most of the coa=
stline is dominated by semidiurnal tides, but some areas such as the <a =
href=3D"/wiki/South_China_Sea" title=3D"South China Sea">South China Sea=
</a> and the <a href=3D"/wiki/Gulf_of_Mexico" title=3D"Gulf of Mexico">G=
ulf of Mexico</a> are primarily diurnal. In the semidiurnal areas, the p=
rimary constituents <i>M</i><sub>2</sub>&#160;(lunar) and <i>S</i><sub>2=
</sub>&#160;(solar) periods differ slightly, so that the relative phases=
, and thus the amplitude of the combined tide, change fortnightly (14&#1=
60;day period).<sup id=3D"cite_ref-32" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"#c=
ite_note-32"><span>[</span>33<span>]</span></a></sup></p>
<p>In the <i>M</i><sub>2</sub> plot above each cotidal line differs by o=
ne hour from its neighbors, and the thicker lines show tides in phase wi=
th equilibrium at Greenwich. The lines rotate around the <a href=3D"/wik=
i/Amphidromic_point" title=3D"Amphidromic point">amphidromic points</a> =
counterclockwise in the northern hemisphere so that from Baja California=
 to Alaska and from France to Ireland the <i>M</i><sub>2</sub> tide prop=
agates northward. In the southern hemisphere this direction is clockwise=
. On the other hand <i>M</i><sub>2</sub> tide propagates counterclockwis=
e around New Zealand, but this because the islands act as a dam and perm=
it the tides to have different heights on opposite sides of the islands.=
 (But the tides do propagate northward on the east side and southward on=
 the west coast, as predicted by theory.)</p>
<p>The exception is at <a href=3D"/wiki/Cook_Strait" title=3D"Cook Strai=
t">Cook Strait</a> where the tidal currents periodically link high to lo=
w water. This is because cotidal lines 180=C2=B0 around the amphidromes =
are in opposite phase, for example high water across from low water at e=
ach end of Cook Strait. Each tidal constituent has a different pattern o=
f amplitudes, phases, and amphidromic points, so the <i>M</i><sub>2</sub=
> patterns cannot be used for other tide components.</p>
