<p>Figure 9 shows the common pattern of two tidal peaks in a day, though=
 remember that the repeat time is not exactly twelve hours (correspondin=
g to the Earth's rotation alone) but 12.4206 hours. The extra time is ne=
eded for a spot on the Earth's surface to catch up with the position dir=
ectly below the moon which has moved onwards also: the moon orbits the e=
arth in the same sense as the earth rotates. Thus the times of high wate=
r, low water, etc. are later every day. Careful attention to frames of r=
eference is needed: all can see that both the sun and the moon cross the=
 sky from east to west, but this is because the earth beneath our feet i=
s rotating from west to east. The moon also orbits the earth in the same=
 sense, west to east, so that one day after seeing moonset, the moon wil=
l stand a little above the horizon due to its own motion and so the eart=
h will have to rotate further to bring on moonset. So the time of moonse=
t is later with reference to the day, which is referenced to the sun's p=
osition, which takes a year to orbit the earth while the moon takes abou=
t a month.</p>
<p>The two peaks are not equal: the twin tidal bulges beneath the Moon a=
nd on the far side of the Earth are aligned with the Moon. Bridgeport is=
 north of the equator, so when the Moon is north of the equator also and=
 shining upon Bridgeport, Bridgeport is closer to its maximum effect tha=
n approximately twelve hours later when Bridgeport is on the far side of=
 the Earth from the Moon and the high tide bulge at Bridgeport's longitu=
de has its maximum south of the equator. Thus the two high tides a day a=
lternate in maximum heights: lower high (just under three feet), higher =
high (just over three feet), and again. Likewise for the low tides.</p>
<p>Figure 10 shows the spring tide / neap tide cycle in the amplitudes o=
f the tides as the Moon orbits the Earth from being in line (Sun=E2=80=93=
Earth=E2=80=93Moon, or Sun=E2=80=93Moon=E2=80=93Earth) when the two main=
 influences combine to give the spring tides, to when the two forces are=
 opposing each other as when the angle Moon=E2=80=93Earth=E2=80=93Sun is=
 close to ninety degrees producing the neap tides. Note also as the Moon=
 moves around its orbit it also changes from north of the equator to sou=
th of the equator. The alternation in the heights of the high tides beco=
mes smaller, until they are the same (the Moon is above the equator), th=
en redevelops but with the other polarity, waxing to a maximum differenc=
e and then waning again.</p>
<p>Figure 11 shows just over a year's worth of tidal height calculations=
. The Sun also cycles between being north or south of the equator and as=
 well the Earth=E2=80=93Sun and Earth=E2=80=93Moon distances change on t=
heir own cycles. None of the various cycle periods are commensurate.</p>=

<p><i>Remember always</i> that calculated tidal heights take no account =
of weather effects, nor include any changes to conditions since the coef=
ficients were determined, such as movement of sandbanks or dredging, etc=
.</p>
<p><a name=3D"Current" id=3D"Current"></a></p>
<h3><span class=3D"editsection">[<a href=3D"/w/index.php?title=3DTide&am=
p;action=3Dedit&amp;section=3D15" title=3D"Edit section: Current">edit</=
a>]</span> <span class=3D"mw-headline">Current</span></h3>
<p>The flow pattern caused by tidal influence is much more difficult to =
analyse, and also, data is <i>much</i> more difficult to collect. A tida=
l height is a simple number which applies to a wide region simultaneousl=
y=E2=80=94often as far as the eye can see. A flow has both a magnitude a=
nd a direction, which both can vary substantially over just a short dist=
ance due to local bathymetry, and also vary with depth below the water s=
urface. Also, although the centre of a channel is the most useful measur=
ing site, mariners will not accept a current measuring installation obst=
ructing navigation, so a flexible approach is required. A flow proceedin=
g up a curved channel is the same flow, even though its direction varies=
 continuously along the channel. But contrary even to the obvious expect=
ation, flood and ebb flows are often not in opposite directions. The dir=
ection of a flow is determined by the shape of the channel it is coming =
from, not the shape where it will shortly be. Likewise, <a href=3D"/wiki=
/Eddies" title=3D"Eddies" class=3D"mw-redirect">eddies</a> can form in o=
ne direction but not the other.</p>
<p>Nevertheless, analysis of currents proceeds on the same basis as tide=
s: At a given location in the simple case, the great majority of the flo=
od flow will be in one direction, and the ebb flow in another (not neces=
sarily opposite) direction. The velocities measured along the flood dire=
ction are taken as positive, and along the ebb direction as negative, an=
d analysis proceeds as if these were tide height figures.</p>
<p>In more complex situations, the flow will not be dominated by the mai=
n ebb and flow directions, with the flow direction and magnitude tracing=
 out an ellipse over a tidal cycle (on a polar plot) instead of along th=
e two lines of ebb and flow direction. In this case, analysis might proc=
eed along two pairs of directions, the primary flow directions and the s=
econdary directions at right angles. Alternatively, the tidal flows can =
be treated as complex numbers, as each value has both a magnitude and a =
direction.</p>
<p>Tide flow information is most commonly seen on <a href=3D"/wiki/Nauti=
cal_chart" title=3D"Nautical chart">nautical charts</a>, presented as a =
table of flow speeds and bearings at hourly intervals, with one set of f=
igures for spring tide and a second for neap tides. The timing is referr=
ed to the times of high water at some harbour where the tidal behaviour =
is similar in pattern, though it may be far distant. As with tide height=
 predictions, tide flow predictions based only on astronomical factors d=
o not take account of weather conditions, which can <i>completely</i> ch=
ange the situation.</p>
<p>The tidal flow through <a href=3D"/wiki/Cook_Strait" title=3D"Cook St=
rait">Cook Strait</a> between the two main islands of New Zealand is par=
ticularly interesting, as on each side of the strait the tide is almost =
exactly out of phase so that high water on one side meets low water on t=
he other. Strong currents result, with almost zero tidal height change i=
n the centre of the strait. Yet, although the tidal surge should flow in=
 one direction for six hours and then the reverse direction for six hour=
s, a particular surge might last eight or ten hours with the reverse sur=
ge enfeebled. In especially boisterous weather conditions, the reverse s=
urge might be entirely overcome so that the flow remains in the same dir=
ection through three surge periods and longer. A further complication fo=
r Cook Strait's pattern of current flow is that the tides at the north e=
nd (e.g. at <a href=3D"/wiki/Nelson,_New_Zealand" title=3D"Nelson, New Z=
ealand">Nelson</a>) have the ordinary two cycles of spring=E2=80=93neap =
tides in a month (as found along the west side of the country), but the =
south end's tidal pattern has only <i>one</i> cycle of spring=E2=80=93ne=
ap tides a month, as found on the east side of the country: <a href=3D"/=
wiki/Wellington,_New_Zealand" title=3D"Wellington, New Zealand" class=3D=
"mw-redirect">Wellington</a>, and <a href=3D"/wiki/Napier,_New_Zealand" =
title=3D"Napier, New Zealand">Napier</a>.</p>
<p>Figure 12 shows separately the height and time of high water and of l=
ow water, through November 2007; these are <i>not</i> measured values bu=
t instead are as produced by calculation from the tidal parameters deriv=
ed from measurements that were made years previously. The nautical chart=
 for Cook Strait offers tidal current information, for instance at 41=C2=
=B013=C2=B79=E2=80=99S 174=C2=B029=C2=B76=E2=80=99E (north west of <a hr=
ef=3D"/wiki/Cape_Terawhiti" title=3D"Cape Terawhiti">Cape Terawhiti</a>)=
 with the January 1979 issue referring timings to <a href=3D"/wiki/Westp=
ort,_New_Zealand" title=3D"Westport, New Zealand">Westport</a> (which ha=
s two spring tides per month, as does Nelson) but the January 2004 issue=
 refers the timings to Wellington, which has one spring tide per month. =
Near Cape Terawhiti in the middle of Cook Strait the tidal height variat=
ion is almost nil while the tidal current reaches its maximum, especiall=
y in the area of the notorious Karori Rip. Aside from weather effects, t=
he actual currents through Cook Strait would be influenced by the tidal =
height differences between the two ends of the strait and as can be seen=
, only one of the two spring tides at the north end (Nelson) has a count=
erpart spring tide at the south end (Wellington), so the resulting behav=
iour would follow neither reference harbour.</p>
<p><a name=3D"Power_generation" id=3D"Power_generation"></a></p>
<h3><span class=3D"editsection">[<a href=3D"/w/index.php?title=3DTide&am=
p;action=3Dedit&amp;section=3D16" title=3D"Edit section: Power generatio=
n">edit</a>]</span> <span class=3D"mw-headline">Power generation</span><=
/h3>
<div class=3D"rellink noprint relarticle mainarticle">Main article: <a h=
ref=3D"/wiki/Tidal_power" title=3D"Tidal power">Tidal power</a></div>
<p>Power can be extracted by two means: inserting a water <a href=3D"/wi=
ki/Turbine" title=3D"Turbine">turbine</a> into a tidal current, or build=
ing impoundment ponds so as to release or admit water through a turbine.=
 In the first case, the generation is entirely determined by the timing =
and magnitude of the tidal currents, and the best currents may be unavai=
lable because the turbines would obstruct navigation. In the second, the=
 impoundment dams are expensive to construct, the natural water cycles a=
re completely disrupted, as is navigation, but with multiple impoundment=
 ponds power can be generated at chosen times. So far, there are few sys=
tems for tidal power generation (most famously, <a href=3D"/wiki/Rance_t=
idal_power_plant" title=3D"Rance tidal power plant">La Rance</a> by <a h=
ref=3D"/wiki/St_Malo" title=3D"St Malo" class=3D"mw-redirect">Saint Malo=
</a>, France) and many difficulties. Aside from environmental issues, si=
mply withstanding sea-water corrosion and fouling by biological growths =
poses engineering challenges.</p>
<p>Proponents of tidal power systems point out that, unlike wind power s=
ystems, the generation pattern can be predicted years ahead. However, we=
ather effects are still problematic. Another assertion is that some gene=
ration is possible for most of the tidal cycle. This may be true in prin=
ciple since the time of still water is short, but in practice turbines l=
ose efficiency at partial operating powers. Since the power available fr=
om a flow is proportional to the cube of the flow speed, the times durin=
g which high power generation is possible turn out to be rather brief.</=
p>
<p><a name=3D"Navigation" id=3D"Navigation"></a></p>
<h2><span class=3D"editsection">[<a href=3D"/w/index.php?title=3DTide&am=
p;action=3Dedit&amp;section=3D17" title=3D"Edit section: Navigation">edi=
t</a>]</span> <span class=3D"mw-headline">Navigation</span></h2>
<div class=3D"thumb tleft">
<div class=3D"thumbinner" style=3D"width:427px;"><a href=3D"/wiki/File:T=
ide_legal_use.gif" class=3D"image" title=3D"Fig. 13: Civil and maritime =
uses of tidal data"><img alt=3D"" src=3D"http://upload.wikimedia.org/wik=
ipedia/en/1/19/Tide_legal_use.gif" width=3D"425" height=3D"282" class=3D=
"thumbimage" /></a>
<div class=3D"thumbcaption">
<div class=3D"magnify"><a href=3D"/wiki/File:Tide_legal_use.gif" class=3D=
"internal" title=3D"Enlarge"><img src=3D"/skins-1.5/common/images/magnif=
y-clip.png" width=3D"15" height=3D"11" alt=3D"" /></a></div>
<i>Fig. 13:</i> Civil and maritime uses of tidal data</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>Tidal flows are of importance in navigation, and significant errors i=
n position will occur if they are not taken into account. Tidal heights =
are also important; for example many rivers and harbours have a shallow =
"bar" at the entrance which will prevent boats with significant <a href=3D=
"/wiki/Draft_(hull)" title=3D"Draft (hull)">draft</a> from entering at c=
ertain states of the tide.</p>
<p>Competence in calculating the effect of tides was important to naval =
officers. The certificate of examination for lieutenants in the <a href=3D=
"/wiki/Royal_Navy" title=3D"Royal Navy">Royal Navy</a> declared that the=
 prospective officer was able to "shift his tides".<sup id=3D"cite_ref-3=
3" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"#cite_note-33"><span>[</span>34<span>]=
</span></a></sup></p>
<p>The timings and velocities of tidal flow can be found by looking at a=
 <i>tidal chart</i> or <a href=3D"/wiki/Tidal_stream_atlas" title=3D"Tid=
al stream atlas" class=3D"mw-redirect">tidal stream atlas</a> for the ar=
ea of interest. Tidal charts come in sets, with each diagram of the set =
covering a single hour between one high water and another (they ignore t=
he extra 24 minutes) and give the average tidal flow for that one hour. =
An arrow on the tidal chart indicates the direction and the average flow=
 speed (usually in <a href=3D"/wiki/Knot_(speed)" title=3D"Knot (speed)"=
 class=3D"mw-redirect">knots</a>) for spring and neap tides. If a tidal =
chart is not available, most nautical charts have "<a href=3D"/wiki/Tida=
l_diamond" title=3D"Tidal diamond">tidal diamonds</a>" which relate spec=
ific points on the chart to a table of data giving direction and speed o=
f tidal flow.</p>
<p>Standard procedure to counteract the effects of tides on navigation i=
s to (1) calculate a "<a href=3D"/wiki/Dead_reckoning" title=3D"Dead rec=
koning">dead reckoning</a>" position (or DR) from distance and direction=
 of travel, (2) mark this on the chart (with a vertical cross like a plu=
s sign) and (3) draw a line from the DR in the direction of the tide. Th=
e distance the tide will have moved the boat along this line is computed=
 by the tidal speed, and this gives an "estimated position" or EP (tradi=
tionally marked with a dot in a triangle).</p>
<p><a href=3D"/wiki/Nautical_chart" title=3D"Nautical chart">Nautical ch=
arts</a> display the "charted depth" of the water at specific locations =
with "<a href=3D"/wiki/Soundings" title=3D"Soundings" class=3D"mw-redire=
ct">soundings</a>" and the use of bathymetric <a href=3D"/wiki/Contour_l=
ine" title=3D"Contour line">contour lines</a> to depict the shape of the=
 submerged surface. These depths are relative to a "<a href=3D"/wiki/Cha=
rt_datum" title=3D"Chart datum">chart datum</a>", which is typically the=
 level of water at the lowest possible astronomical tide (tides may be l=
ower or higher for meteorological reasons) and are therefore the minimum=
 water depth possible during the tidal cycle. "Drying heights" may also =
be shown on the chart, which are the heights of the exposed <a href=3D"/=
wiki/Seabed" title=3D"Seabed">seabed</a> at the lowest astronomical tide=
.</p>
<p>Heights and times of low and high water on each day are published in =
tide tables. The actual depth of water at the given points at high or lo=
w water can easily be calculated by adding the charted depth to the publ=
ished height of the tide. The water depth for times other than high or l=
ow water can be derived from <a href=3D"/w/index.php?title=3DTidal_curve=
&amp;action=3Dedit&amp;redlink=3D1" class=3D"new" title=3D"Tidal curve (=
page does not exist)">tidal curves</a> published for major ports. If an =
accurate curve is not available, the <a href=3D"/wiki/Rule_of_twelfths" =
title=3D"Rule of twelfths">rule of twelfths</a> can be used. This approx=
imation works on the basis that the increase in depth in the six hours b=
etween low and high water will follow this simple rule: first hour =E2=80=
=94 1/12, second =E2=80=94 2/12, third =E2=80=94 3/12, fourth =E2=80=94 =
3/12, fifth =E2=80=94 2/12, sixth =E2=80=94 1/12.</p>
<p><a name=3D"Biological_aspects" id=3D"Biological_aspects"></a></p>
<h2><span class=3D"editsection">[<a href=3D"/w/index.php?title=3DTide&am=
p;action=3Dedit&amp;section=3D18" title=3D"Edit section: Biological aspe=
cts">edit</a>]</span> <span class=3D"mw-headline">Biological aspects</sp=
an></h2>
<p><a name=3D"Intertidal_ecology" id=3D"Intertidal_ecology"></a></p>
<h3><span class=3D"editsection">[<a href=3D"/w/index.php?title=3DTide&am=
p;action=3Dedit&amp;section=3D19" title=3D"Edit section: Intertidal ecol=
ogy">edit</a>]</span> <span class=3D"mw-headline">Intertidal ecology</sp=
an></h3>
<div class=3D"thumb tright">
<div class=3D"thumbinner" style=3D"width:202px;"><a href=3D"/wiki/File:I=
ntertide_zonation_at_Kalaloch.jpg" class=3D"image" title=3D"Fig. 14: A r=
ock, seen at low water, exhibiting typical intertidal zonation."><img al=
t=3D"" src=3D"http://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c8/I=
ntertide_zonation_at_Kalaloch.jpg/200px-Intertide_zonation_at_Kalaloch.j=
pg" width=3D"200" height=3D"322" class=3D"thumbimage" /></a>
<div class=3D"thumbcaption">
<div class=3D"magnify"><a href=3D"/wiki/File:Intertide_zonation_at_Kalal=
och.jpg" class=3D"internal" title=3D"Enlarge"><img src=3D"/skins-1.5/com=
mon/images/magnify-clip.png" width=3D"15" height=3D"11" alt=3D"" /></a><=
/div>
<i>Fig. 14:</i> A rock, seen at low water, exhibiting typical intertidal=
 zonation.</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class=3D"rellink noprint relarticle mainarticle">Main article: <a h=
ref=3D"/wiki/Intertidal_ecology" title=3D"Intertidal ecology">Intertidal=
 ecology</a></div>
<p>Intertidal ecology is the study of <a href=3D"/wiki/Intertidal_zone" =
title=3D"Intertidal zone">intertidal</a> <a href=3D"/wiki/Ecosystem" tit=
le=3D"Ecosystem">ecosystems</a>, where organisms live between the low an=
d high water lines. At low water, the intertidal is exposed (or =E2=80=98=
emersed=E2=80=99) whereas at high water, the intertidal is underwater (o=
r =E2=80=98immersed=E2=80=99). Intertidal <a href=3D"/wiki/Ecologist" ti=
tle=3D"Ecologist" class=3D"mw-redirect">ecologists</a> therefore study t=
he interactions between intertidal organisms and their environment, as w=
ell as between <a href=3D"/wiki/Biological_interaction" title=3D"Biologi=
cal interaction">different species</a> of intertidal organisms within a =
particular intertidal community. The most important environmental and sp=
ecies interactions may vary based on the type of intertidal community be=
ing studied, the broadest of classifications being based on substrates =E2=
=80=94 <a href=3D"/wiki/Rocky_shore" title=3D"Rocky shore">rocky shore</=
a> and soft bottom communities.</p>
<p>Organisms living in this zone have a highly variable and often hostil=
e environment, and have evolved various <a href=3D"/wiki/Adaptation" tit=
le=3D"Adaptation">adaptations</a> to cope with and even exploit these co=
nditions. One easily visible feature of intertidal communities is <a hre=
f=3D"/wiki/Vertical_zonation" title=3D"Vertical zonation" class=3D"mw-re=
direct">vertical zonation</a>, where the community is divided into disti=
nct vertical bands of specific species going up the shore. A species' ab=
ility to cope with <a href=3D"/wiki/Desiccation" title=3D"Desiccation">d=
esiccation</a> determines their upper limits, while <a href=3D"/wiki/Com=
petition_(biology)" title=3D"Competition (biology)">competition</a> with=
 other species sets their lower limits.</p>
<p>Intertidal regions are <a href=3D"/wiki/Exploitation_of_natural_resou=
rces" title=3D"Exploitation of natural resources">utilized</a> by humans=
 for food and recreation, but anthropogenic actions also have major impa=
cts, with overexploitation, <a href=3D"/wiki/Invasive_species" title=3D"=
Invasive species">invasive species</a> and <a href=3D"/wiki/Global_warmi=
ng" title=3D"Global warming">climate change</a> being among the problems=
 faced by intertidal communities. In some places <a href=3D"/wiki/Marine=
_Protected_Area" title=3D"Marine Protected Area">Marine Protected Areas<=
/a> have been established to protect these areas and aid in <a href=3D"/=
wiki/Scientific_research" title=3D"Scientific research" class=3D"mw-redi=
rect">scientific research</a>.</p>
<p><a name=3D"Biological_rhythms" id=3D"Biological_rhythms"></a></p>
<h3><span class=3D"editsection">[<a href=3D"/w/index.php?title=3DTide&am=
p;action=3Dedit&amp;section=3D20" title=3D"Edit section: Biological rhyt=
hms">edit</a>]</span> <span class=3D"mw-headline">Biological rhythms</sp=
an></h3>
<p>Intertidal organisms are greatly affected by the approximately fortni=
ghtly cycle of the tides, and hence their <a href=3D"/wiki/Biological_rh=
ythm" title=3D"Biological rhythm" class=3D"mw-redirect">biological rhyth=
ms</a> tend to occur in rough multiples of this period. This is seen not=
 only in the intertidal organisms however, but also in many other terres=
trial animals, such as the <a href=3D"/wiki/Vertebrate" title=3D"Vertebr=
ate">vertebrates</a>. Examples include <a href=3D"/wiki/Gestation" title=
=3D"Gestation">gestation</a> and the hatching of eggs. In humans, for ex=
ample, the <a href=3D"/wiki/Menstrual_cycle" title=3D"Menstrual cycle">m=
enstrual cycle</a> lasts roughly a month, an even multiple of the period=
 of the tidal cycle. This may be evidence of the <a href=3D"/wiki/Common=
_descent" title=3D"Common descent">common descent</a> of all animals fro=
m a marine ancestor.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-Descent_34-0" class=3D"reference=
"><a href=3D"#cite_note-Descent-34"><span>[</span>35<span>]</span></a></=
sup></p>
<p><a name=3D"Other_tides" id=3D"Other_tides"></a></p>
<h2><span class=3D"editsection">[<a href=3D"/w/index.php?title=3DTide&am=
p;action=3Dedit&amp;section=3D21" title=3D"Edit section: Other tides">ed=
it</a>]</span> <span class=3D"mw-headline">Other tides</span></h2>
<p>In addition to oceanic tides, there are <i><a href=3D"/wiki/Atmospher=
ic_tide" title=3D"Atmospheric tide">atmospheric tides</a></i> as well as=
 <i><a href=3D"/wiki/Earth_tide" title=3D"Earth tide">earth tides</a></i=
>. All of these are <a href=3D"/wiki/Continuum_mechanics" title=3D"Conti=
nuum mechanics">continuum mechanical</a> phenomena, the first two being =
<a href=3D"/wiki/Fluid_mechanics" title=3D"Fluid mechanics">fluids</a> a=
nd the third being essentially the thin <a href=3D"/wiki/Solid_mechanics=
" title=3D"Solid mechanics">solid</a> Earth's crust on top of the semi-l=
iquid Earth's interior (with various modifications).</p>
<p>Atmospheric tides are negligible from ground level and aviation altit=
udes, drowned by the much more important effects of <a href=3D"/wiki/Wea=
ther" title=3D"Weather">weather</a>. Atmospheric tides are both gravitat=
ional and thermal in origin and are the dominant dynamics from about 80&=
#160;km to 120&#160;km where the molecular density becomes too small to =
behave as a fluid.</p>
<p>Earth tides or terrestrial tides affect the entire mass of the Earth,=
 which can be viewed as a liquid gyro with a very thin crust. The Earth'=
s crust shifts (up/down, east/west, north/south) in response to the Moon=
's and Sun's gravitation, ocean tides, and atmospheric loading. While ne=
gligible for most human activities, the semidiurnal amplitude of terrest=
rial tides can reach about 55&#160;cm at the equator (15&#160;cm is due =
to the Sun) which is important in <a href=3D"/wiki/GPS" title=3D"GPS" cl=
ass=3D"mw-redirect">GPS</a> calibration and <a href=3D"/wiki/VLBI" title=
=3D"VLBI" class=3D"mw-redirect">VLBI</a> measurements. Also to make prec=
ise astronomical angular measurements requires knowledge of the Earth's =
rate of rotation and <a href=3D"/wiki/Nutation" title=3D"Nutation">nutat=
ion</a>, both of which are influenced by earth tides. The semi-diurnal <=
i>M</i><sub>2</sub> Earth tides are nearly in phase with the Moon with t=
idal lag of about two hours.<sup class=3D"noprint Template-Fact" title=3D=
"This claim needs references to reliable sources from August 2008" style=
=3D"white-space:nowrap;">[<i><a href=3D"/wiki/Wikipedia:Citation_needed"=
 title=3D"Wikipedia:Citation needed">citation needed</a></i>]</sup> Terr=
estrial tides also need to be taken in account in the case of some <a hr=
ef=3D"/wiki/Particle_physics" title=3D"Particle physics">particle physic=
s</a> experiments.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-35" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D=
"#cite_note-35"><span>[</span>36<span>]</span></a></sup></p>
<p>For instance, at the <a href=3D"/wiki/CERN" title=3D"CERN">CERN</a> o=
r <a href=3D"/wiki/SLAC" title=3D"SLAC" class=3D"mw-redirect">SLAC</a>, =
the very large <a href=3D"/wiki/Particle_accelerator" title=3D"Particle =
accelerator">particle accelerators</a> were designed while taking terres=
trial tides into account for proper operation. Among the effects that ne=
ed to be taken into account are circumference deformation for circular a=
ccelerators and particle beam energy.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-36" class=3D"re=
ference"><a href=3D"#cite_note-36"><span>[</span>37<span>]</span></a></s=
up> Since tidal forces generate currents of conducting fluids within the=
 interior of the Earth, they affect in turn the <a href=3D"/wiki/Earth%2=
7s_magnetic_field" title=3D"Earth's magnetic field">Earth's magnetic fie=
ld</a> itself.</p>
<p>When oscillating tidal currents in the stratified ocean flow over une=
ven bottom topography, they generate <a href=3D"/wiki/Internal_waves" ti=
tle=3D"Internal waves" class=3D"mw-redirect">internal waves</a> with tid=
al frequencies. Such waves are called <i>internal tides</i>.</p>
<p>The <i><a href=3D"/wiki/Galactic_tide" title=3D"Galactic tide">galact=
ic tide</a></i> is the tidal force exerted by galaxies on stars within t=
hem and <a href=3D"/wiki/Satellite_galaxies" title=3D"Satellite galaxies=
" class=3D"mw-redirect">satellite galaxies</a> orbiting them. The effect=
s of the galactic tide on the <a href=3D"/wiki/Solar_System" title=3D"So=
lar System">Solar System</a>'s <a href=3D"/wiki/Oort_cloud" title=3D"Oor=
t cloud">Oort cloud</a> are believed to be the cause of 90 percent of al=
l observed long-period comets.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-37" class=3D"reference=
"><a href=3D"#cite_note-37"><span>[</span>38<span>]</span></a></sup></p>=

<p><a name=3D"Misapplications" id=3D"Misapplications"></a></p>
<h2><span class=3D"editsection">[<a href=3D"/w/index.php?title=3DTide&am=
p;action=3Dedit&amp;section=3D22" title=3D"Edit section: Misapplications=
">edit</a>]</span> <span class=3D"mw-headline">Misapplications</span></h=
2>
<p><a href=3D"/wiki/Tsunamis" title=3D"Tsunamis" class=3D"mw-redirect">T=
sunamis</a>, the large waves that occur after earthquakes, are sometimes=
 called <i>tidal waves</i>, but this name is given by their <i>resemblan=
ce</i> to the tide, rather than any actual link to the tide. Other pheno=
mena unrelated to tides but using the word <i>tide</i> are <a href=3D"/w=
iki/Rip_current" title=3D"Rip current">rip tide</a>, <a href=3D"/wiki/St=
orm_tide" title=3D"Storm tide" class=3D"mw-redirect">storm tide</a>, <a =
href=3D"/wiki/Hurricane_tide" title=3D"Hurricane tide" class=3D"mw-redir=
ect">hurricane tide</a>, and <a href=3D"/wiki/Oil_spill" title=3D"Oil sp=
ill">black</a> or <a href=3D"/wiki/Red_tide" title=3D"Red tide">red tide=
s</a>.</p>
