<li id=3D"cite_note-difference-17"><b><a href=3D"#cite_ref-difference_17=
-0">^</a></b> Two points on either side of the Earth sample the imposed =
gravity at two nearby points, effectively providing a finite difference =
of the gravitational force that varies as the inverse square of the dist=
ance. The derivative of 1/<i>r</i><sup>2</sup>, with <i>r</i> =3D distan=
ce to originating body, varies as the inverse cube.</li>
<li id=3D"cite_note-18"><b><a href=3D"#cite_ref-18">^</a></b> Hypothetic=
ally, if the ocean were a constant depth, there were no land, and the Ea=
rth did not rotate, high water would occur as two bulges in the height o=
f the oceans, one facing the Moon and the other on the opposite side of =
the Earth, facing away from the Moon. There would also be smaller, super=
imposed bulges on the sides facing toward and away from the Sun.</li>
